🚀Lesson 2: Containerization & Orchestration (Docker, EKS, Fargate)
You mentioned moving JAR files from Git to ECR and then to K8s.

2.1 The Container Lifecycle
Build: Jenkins clones the code.

Scan: Use Trivy.

Logic: If Trivy finds a CRITICAL vulnerability, exit 1 (Stop the pipeline). If LOW, exit 0 (Continue).

Push: Image is tagged (e.g., v1.0.1) and pushed to Amazon ECR.

Deploy: * EKS (EC2 Launch Type): can manage the worker nodes. Better for custom networking.

EKS (Fargate Profile): "Serverless" Kubernetes. No nodes to manage. AWS handles the scaling.

🚀Lesson 3: The Observability Stack (Logging & Metrics)
You asked about ELK, Prometheus, and Grafana.

3.1 Metrics (Prometheus + Grafana)
Scraping: Prometheus "pulls" data from an endpoint (usually port 9100 for Node Exporter).

Visualizing: Grafana connects to Prometheus. You create "Timelapse" dashboards to see CPU/Memory trends.

CloudWatch Integration: can use the CloudWatch Data Source in Grafana to see RDS or CloudFront metrics without installing agents.

3.2 Logging (EFK: Elasticsearch, Fluent Bit, Kibana)
Why Fluent Bit? In Kubernetes, logs are written to files on the node. Fluent Bit runs as a DaemonSet (one pod per node), reads those files, and ships them to Elasticsearch.

The "Index Hanging" Fix: If your ELK is hanging, it's likely a Shard issue.

Lesson: Don't let indexes grow forever. Use Index Lifecycle Management (ILM) to transition logs from "Hot" (fast SSD) to "Warm" (cheap storage) to "Delete."

🚀Lesson 4: Linux Mastery (Permissions & Delimiters)
4.1 The 722 Permission Secret
You need to know the "3 sections" for your interview:

User (7): Read(4) + Write(2) + Execute(1) = Full Control.

Group (2): Write(2) = Can only modify, cannot see or run.

Others (2): Write(2) = Can only modify.

4.2 The "Big Three" Text Tools
cut -d ':' -f 1 /etc/passwd: Splits a line by the colon : and takes the 1st part (the username).

awk '{print $1}': Splits by whitespace automatically. Great for logs.

sed -i 's/old/new/g' file.txt: Finds every instance of "old" and replaces it with "new" directly in the file (the -i stands for "in-place").

🚀Lesson 5: GitOps & Deployment Strategies
5.1 Argo CD (The Pull Model)
Traditional Jenkins "pushes" code to the server. Argo CD "pulls" it.

Advantage: If someone manually changes a setting in Kubernetes (Configuration Drift), Argo CD will detect it and automatically "Sync" it back to what is in Git.

5.2 Deployment Types
Blue-Green: Two identical environments. Switch 100% traffic to Green. Safe but expensive (2x resources).

Canary: Small percentage (5%) gets the new version. If logs (in ELK) look good, roll out to 100%.
